$[?25h]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◑ Lighthouse keeper story]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story]133;C]133;D]0;✳ Lighthouse keeper story[?25l[6;1Hclaude: The Last Winter at Carrick Point[K
[K
For thirty-one years, Agnes Halloran had climbed the spiral of the Carrick Point light at dusk. The climb never changed: one hundred and twelve iron steps, a landing where the wind found every crack in the glass, then the lamp room with its
 great lens turning on a bath of mercury. She knew the count the way other women knew their prayers. Lately her knees kept their own count, and it was louder than the sea.[K
[K
The letter came in October. Two pages from the Northern Lights Board, signed by a man in an office four hundred miles south who had never seen the rock. The light would be converted to a solar beacon in the spring. The keeper's cottage
would be sold. Her pension was generous, the letter said, in the careful way such letters are generous, and she was thanked for her service.[14;1HShe read it twice at the kitchen table, folded it into quarters, and put it in the drawer with the spare matches.[16;1HHer nephew Daniel wanted her in Portland. He called on Sundays, bright and anxious, and described a room with a view of a parking structure. "It's not so bad, Aunt Agnes. Things have to change." She said yes, things change. She did not tell
 him that she had changed things herself every night for thirty-one years, by climbing the stairs.[19;1HThe storm arrived on the second Tuesday of November. It came the way the old keepers had described to her when she was a girl, not as wind but as weight, pressing the shutters inward and filling the cottage with a sound like a freight train
 that never reached the station. By six o'clock she could not see the boathouse from the window.[22;1HAt seven the radio crackled. A fishing boat, the Marguerite, four men aboard, was taking water somewhere off the Teeth, the reef that had put more ships on the bottom than any other stretch of coast. The helicopter at Harrow Bay was
grounded by the ceiling. The lifeboat could not launch in that swell.[25;1H"Carrick, do you have a light? Anything you can give us."[27;1H"The light's on," Agnes said. "It's always on."[29;1H"Copy that. Keep it on."[31;1HShe pulled her oilskin from the hook and her boots from under the bed. Her hands did not shake, which surprised her.[33;1HThe climb was worse than she remembered. The wind came through the gallery door in flat, violent shoves, and twice she stopped on the landing with her forehead against the cold rail, breathing, counting. At the top, the lamp room was warm
from the lamp, a yellow-white heart beating inside its crystal. The clockwork ran on falling weights and needed winding every four hours. She had wound it at noon. The next winding was due in an hour, and she did not know whether the
mechanism would hold through a night of unbroken use. The new automated station had been designed to be watched by a machine. This lamp had been built for a person.[37;1HShe polished the glass where salt had begun to bloom and sat down on the stool beside the pedestal. Through the panes there was nothing but darkness and, far out, the faint white flash of breaking water. Then, after a long time, a second
light appeared: a small, unsteady yellow spark low on the swell, coming closer.[40;1H"We see you, Carrick," the radio said. The voice was younger now, shaky with relief. "We have you on the chart. Keep it burning."[42;1H"I'm not going anywhere," Agnes said.[44;1HIt took the Marguerite two hours to find the channel. Agnes did not leave the lamp room. She wound the clockwork at eleven and again at three. Around one o'clock the wind dropped for almost a minute, and in that silence she heard the boat's
 engine cough and catch, and then the rescue crew's voices across the water, faint and clear, like people calling from another room.[47;1HAt dawn the storm blew itself out in a gray exhaustion. The sea lay heaving and battered, but it was only the sea again. The cottage roof was missing a row of shingles, and the boathouse door hung from one hinge. Across the bay, the
Marguerite rode at anchor behind the harbor wall, four men on her deck waving at the tower.[50;1HAgnes lifted one hand from the stool and waved back.]0;◐ Lighthouse keeper story[?25h

Later that morning a coast guard officer came up the stairs, breathless and red-faced, and asked what she wanted him to tell the board.

She thought of the letter in the drawer. She thought of the solar panel that would someday blink on this rock with no one to see it, and of the men on the Marguerite, who would go home to their families and never learn the name of the woman[50;240H
 who kept the lamp.

"Tell them the light was on," she said. "Tell them it was on all night."

He wrote it down. Then he offered her his arm on the stairs, and she took it, because for once the count was not hers to keep.
